    {
      "id": 13,
      "type": "text",
      "title": "How the AI Tax Classifier Works",
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 5,
        "w": 24,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 41
      },
      "options": {
        "content": "### AI Tax Bracket Classification\n\n**What it does:** Given a user's income, state, age, credential, school type, filing status, debt, experience, and dependents, the neural network classifies them into one of 7 federal income tax brackets.\n\n**Architecture:** 3-layer MLP — Input(61) → Dense(128, ReLU) → Dense(64, ReLU) → Dense(32, ReLU) → Output(7, Softmax) — **18,503 total parameters** — 95.4% accuracy on held-out test set.\n\n**Features used (61 total):** log(income) · age/75 · years_exp/40 · log(student_debt) · log(monthly_payment) · log(median_earnings) · log(median_debt) · debt_to_earnings · dependents/5 · retirement_rate · 40 state one-hot flags · 3 school type flags · 3 credential flags · 3 filing status flags · homeowner flag · part-time flag\n\n**Training data:** 10,000 synthetic profiles with income drawn from realistic log-normal distributions per credential level, with experience and age premiums applied. All labels assigned using the deterministic 2024 IRS bracket schedule.\n\n⚠️ Disclaimer: This classifier is trained on synthetic data for educational demonstration. For real tax planning, use IRS-certified software or consult a tax professional.",
        "mode": "markdown"
      }
    }
